/**
 * @mainpage FlightOS
 *
 * @section intro Introduction
 *
 * FlightOS (formerly LeanOS) is a lightweight operating system for
 * space-instrumentation software. The repository is maintained by the space
 * instrumentation working group at the Department of Astrophysics of the
 * University of Vienna. Its current implementation target is the SPARC v8
 * family, primarily the LEON3-based GR712RC and GR740. The source tree also
 * contains legacy support for the NoC and Xentium DSPs associated with the
 * MPPB/SSDP processing platforms.
 *
 * The project grew out of evaluation work on MPPB hardware and was later
 * developed around the SSDP concept. SSDP itself was abandoned, but the NoC
 * and DSP support remained modular parts of the operating-system codebase.
 * The repository contains a requirements specification, architectural design
 * description, user manual, performance report, and test documentation under
 * `Documentation/OS`; the source and the selected build configuration
 * together determine the facilities provided by any given image.
 *
 * @section scope Scope and status
 *
 * FlightOS is a target-specific operating system rather than a general-purpose
 * or fully portable operating system. The top-level build defaults to `sparc`,
 * and `arch/sparc` is the only architecture directory currently present. The
 * SPARC configuration provides LEON2, LEON3, and LEON4 selections; GR712RC
 * and GR740 are the primary target platforms. The implementation is primarily
 * C, with SPARC assembly used for machine-level entry, context switching,
 * traps, and other target-specific interfaces.
 *
 * Functionality is selected at build time through a modified Linux kbuild
 * system and Kconfig. The set of drivers, memory features, modules, DSP
 * programs, samples, and startup code depends on the selected configuration.
 * A build normally links the architecture code, common kernel, initialization
 * code, projects, and common libraries into the `flightos` image. Optional
 * modules and embedded archive images add further configuration-dependent
 * behavior.
 *
 * FlightOS targets unmanned space applications at Software Criticality Level C
 * or higher. The kernel is lightweight, with limited external dependencies,
 * POSIX-oriented interfaces where applicable, modularity, and
 * resource-conscious operation. The degree to which individual requirements
 * are met depends on the selected configuration and its implementation.
 *
 * @section architecture Architectural design
 *
 * The architecture is organized into the following software boundaries:
 *
 * - User space contains applications and the C library. The library provides
 *   the user-facing wrappers that reach kernel functionality through system
 *   calls where such wrappers are implemented.
 * - The kernel-facing system-call and control interfaces form the boundary to
 *   privileged services. The system-control (`sysctl`) interface is a logical
 *   tree of character-oriented entries registered by kernel components; entries
 *   may expose configuration, state, statistics, or component-defined binary
 *   data.
 * - Architecture-independent kernel code in `kernel/` and reusable support in
 *   `lib/` implement policies and generic data structures.
 * - Architecture code in `arch/sparc/` supplies boot, trap and interrupt entry,
 *   processor state, memory and MMU operations, timers, I/O, and other LEON or
 *   SPARC-specific mechanisms.
 *
 * The source implements this boundary through generic headers under `include/`
 * and architecture headers under `arch/sparc/include/`. Generic services call
 * abstract operations supplied by the selected architecture rather than
 * directly embedding hardware register access. A new architecture must provide
 * the interfaces required by its selected configuration; the source-level
 * separation does not make every component portable without such a backend.
 *
 * The dependency direction is from higher-level services toward lower
 * abstraction layers. Hardware-specific details stay in architecture code or
 * driver backends; generic scheduling, timing, interrupt, memory, and driver
 * contracts remain usable by their consumers. The design favors component
 * encapsulation and leaves detailed implementation choices in the source so
 * that the codebase can evolve without changing its fundamental component
 * boundaries.
 *
 * @subsection boot Boot and initialization
 *
 * The architecture setup path initializes the platform before the common
 * kernel initialization path promotes the boot context to the main kernel
 * task. Depending on configuration, startup uses compiler-provided or
 * architecture-provided boot code. The initialization code runs registered
 * initcalls, optionally loads an embedded module image, optionally runs the
 * Xentium demonstration, and then enters the main kernel loop. A custom boot
 * configuration can also invoke registered user-call functions. The exact
 * user-space or application startup sequence is configuration- and
 * platform-dependent.
 *
 * @subsection protection Protection and failure handling
 *
 * SPARC trap entry provides the transition into privileged handling for
 * interrupts, exceptions, and system calls. When enabled, the SRMMU supplies
 * virtual mappings and user/supervisor memory protection. The kernel provides
 * logging, `BUG`/panic-style halt paths, reboot support, and architecture trap
 * handlers. Halt and reboot carry a reason code from `include/kernel/reboot.h`
 * (for example `REBOOT_WATCHDOG`, `REBOOT_EDAC`, `REBOOT_OUT_OF_MEM`); the
 * architecture records this code and transitions to the platform-specific
 * reset sequence. Safe-mode and recovery extensions are reserved for future
 * work.
 *
 * @section components Major components
 *
 * @subsection scheduling Scheduling and threads
 *
 * `kernel/sched/` and `kernel/kthread.c` manage task control blocks, task
 * states, stacks, CPU placement, synchronization, and context-switch requests.
 * Round-robin and earliest-deadline-first scheduler policies are provided. SMP
 * support, CPU affinity, task migration, and cross-CPU scheduler state are
 * available through the configured scheduler and architecture paths. SPARC
 * thread setup and context switching are supplied by
 * `arch/sparc/kernel/thread.c` and the related assembly/layout headers. SMP
 * configuration, affinity, deadlines, preemption, and cleanup behavior depend
 * on the selected policy and configuration. Some areas—such as FPU lazy
 * switching and complete SMP CPU-id handling—remain under active development.
 *
 * See @ref schedthread, @ref schedsys, and @ref threadsys.
 *
 * @subsection timing Timing
 *
 * `kernel/time.c`, `clockevent.c`, `tick.c`, and `watchdog.c` define generic
 * timekeeping, clocksource, clockevent, tick, and watchdog abstractions.
 * SPARC LEON code supplies timer register access and the hardware backend.
 * The timing implementation supports periodic and one-shot/tickless operation
 * where selected by the implementation and configuration. The current SPARC
 * backends include LEON3/LEON4-specific uptime sources and general-purpose
 * timer clockevents. Clocksource readout overhead and the minimum clockevent
 * period are calibrated during initialization. Timing measurements for
 * GR712 configurations are available in the performance report under
 * `Documentation/OS/performance/`.
 * See @ref timing and @ref time.
 *
 * @subsection interrupts Interrupts and traps
 *
 * `kernel/irq.c` defines the generic interrupt request and controller contract.
 * The SPARC backend connects trap-table entry to primary and extended interrupt
 * dispatch, immediate handlers, and deferred work. Interrupt statistics and
 * protection options are Kconfig-controlled. The subsystem uses fixed resource
 * pools for handler registration and deferred work; concurrency guarantees
 * between CPUs are determined by the selected implementation and
 * configuration. The interrupt subsystem pages contain detailed descriptions
 * of the handler vectors, dispatch paths, and locking areas.
 *
 * See @ref interrupts and @ref irqsys.
 *
 * @subsection memory Memory management
 *
 * Memory management is layered from architecture boot memory and physical page
 * discovery through reusable buddy/page/chunk managers and the kernel heap.
 * `kernel/kmem.c` and the memory code in `lib/` provide the generic layers;
 * `arch/sparc/kernel/bootmem.c`, `mm.c`, `mmu.c`, and `arch/sparc/mm/` provide
 * the target realization. SRMMU mapping is optional and is available through
 * the SPARC SRMMU support. The code also contains memory scrubbing (including
 * full-roundtrip statistics) and EDAC interfaces, with SPARC LEON
 * memory-controller support. The generic EDAC layer exposes error checking,
 * critical memory regions, error-state handling, synthetic fault injection,
 * and bypass reads; the architecture backend translates these into LEON
 * memory-controller actions. Configuration controls the allocator layers and
 * release behavior.
 *
 * See @ref kmem, @ref buddy_mm, @ref page, @ref chunk, @ref srmmu,
 * and @ref edacsys.
 *
 * @subsection drivers Drivers and hardware services
 *
 * Driver code in `kernel/` exposes generic or device-facing interfaces while
 * relying on SPARC LEON I/O, interrupt, cache, address, and DMA helpers. The
 * current driver families include:
 *
 * - SPI controller and device support, including GRSPI, AD7814, and
 *   ADC128S102 components.
 * - GRSPW2 SpaceWire packet and routing support.
 * - MPPB/SSDP NoC two-dimensional DMA with a configurable transfer queue.
 * - Xentium host-side loading, mailbox command exchange, and processing-network
 *   scheduling.
 *
 * GRSPW2 support, SPI controller infrastructure, and GRSPI support are
 * independent device concerns. Drivers are enabled independently in Kconfig
 * where applicable. Hardware address and DMA assumptions are target-specific,
 * even when public headers are generic. See @ref drivers, @ref spi_driver,
 * @ref grspw2, @ref noc_dma, and @ref xentium_driver.
 *
 * @subsection processing Processing networks and Xentium DSPs
 *
 * `lib/data_proc_task.c`, `data_proc_tracker.c`, and `data_proc_net.c` provide
 * a generic task/tracker/network model. A processing task carries a data buffer
 * and an ordered list of operation identifiers. Tracker nodes associate an
 * operation identifier with a processing function and a critical pending-task
 * level. The network routes a task to matching nodes and ultimately to a
 * user-defined output node. This supports per-task routes, repeated operation
 * identifiers, and processing functions that manipulate task steps, subject to
 * the interfaces and ownership rules in the source.
 *
 * The Xentium driver maps the same model to loaded Xentium ELF programs. NoC
 * DMA and mailbox messages transfer work between the host and DSP. DSP program
 * sources and support libraries live under `dsp/xentium/`; Kconfig can build
 * them and optionally embed generated images in an archive. The resulting
 * pipelines are assembled from configuration-selected kernels and boot
 * behavior.
 *
 * @subsection modules Modules, images, and symbols
 *
 * The ELF loader, archive support, symbol table, and module code implement the
 * loadable-module path. Modules may be built into the image or represented as
 * relocatable ELF objects; Kconfig also supports embedding an archive of
 * generated modules and other payload data. The module image is loaded by
 * initialization code when enabled. Refer to `kernel/module.c`,
 * `kernel/elf_loader.c`, `kernel/ksym.c`, `lib/ar.c`, and their documented
 * interfaces for the exact supported object format and lifecycle.
 *
 * The application loader in `kernel/application.c` accepts an ELF image in
 * memory, loads its runtime sections, applies supported relocations, resolves
 * the `_start` symbol, and starts the application in a kernel thread with the
 * requested CPU affinity and argument vector. Relocatable images are allocated
 * by the kernel. Executable images currently use their encoded addresses; a
 * general relocation or reserved-memory mechanism for such images is not
 * implemented.
 *
 * @subsection libc C library and applications
 *
 * `libc/` is a separate SPARC-targeted library tree containing string, memory,
 * formatted-I/O, time, threading, signal, scheduling, sysctl, and device
 * wrapper sources. Not all of POSIX or a hosted C runtime is present; the
 * implemented functions and wrappers define the scope. The architecture
 * supports a small, POSIX-oriented user API where applicable, with
 * supporting C-library functionality in user space.
 *
 * @subsection testing Testing and examples
 *
 * Examples are under `samples/`. Host-side unit tests, mocks, and hardware-test
 * scripts are under `tools/testing/`. The testing approach uses an adapted
 * kselftest-style framework with human-readable results and shell exit status.
 * Unit/integration tests, hardware-software tests, and acceptance tests are
 * covered, with hardware constraints specified per test. Testing is incremental
 * and version-dependent; compliance is only partially verified against the
 * current test tree. The top-level `make unittest` target builds and runs the
 * unit-test collection.
 *
 * @section requirements Motivation and requirements
 *
 * FlightOS addresses the management of LEON host resources and, as legacy
 * platform functionality, a NoC-connected collection of Xentium DSPs for
 * on-board astronomical data processing. Following the abandonment of the SSDP
 * project, the operating-system concept was generalized to the LEON3 family of
 * European space processors. The design uses modular processing components,
 * explicit DMA and data-buffer management, and configuration-specific builds
 * rather than a broad general-purpose feature set.
 *
 * The principal design concerns are:
 *
 * - SPARC/LEON boot, supervisor-mode separation, traps, interrupts, timers,
 *   memory management, SMP, and optional SRMMU support.
 * - Kernel tasks with priorities, synchronization, message queues, and
 *   scheduling policies including fixed-priority and EDF operation.
 * - A narrow system-call boundary, user-space library support, configurable
 *   initialization, and a generic system-control tree.
 * - Modular device support, application ELF loading, and loadable/embedded ELF
 *   content, including GRSPW2, SPI, NoC, SpaceWire, and Xentium functionality.
 * - Resource-conscious implementation, source-level interface documentation,
 *   configuration through kbuild, and tests/examples maintained in the tree.
 *
 * Performance, safety, reliability, and verification objectives are part of
 * the design scope. The source and the detailed Doxygen pages identify
 * incomplete or experimental areas, including portions of SMP handling, timer
 * policy, interrupt resource locking and protection, and error/recovery
 * behavior. No stronger behavior is described here than what the current code
 * establishes.
 *
 * @section navigation Documentation navigation
 *
 * The generated Doxygen pages document interfaces alongside the source. The
 * subsystem groups linked above provide entry points to the relevant headers in
 * `include/` and matching implementation or architecture backends. The
 * requirements specification, architectural design description, user manual,
 * performance report, project assurance plan, and test documentation are in
 * `Documentation/OS/`; their LaTeX sources and pre-rendered PDFs use the
 * historical LeanOS name in places.
 *
 * To generate HTML source documentation, install Doxygen and PlantUML and run
 * `make` in `Documentation/doxygen`. To configure and build the target image,
 * use `make menuconfig` followed by `make` at the repository root, with the
 * appropriate SPARC cross-toolchain and target configuration.
 *
 * @section disclaimer Disclaimer
 *
 * Parts of this Doxygen documentation have been summarised or updated with the
 * assistance of large language models. The descriptions should be treated as a
 * guide to the code rather than as an authoritative specification. No code of
 * FlightOS has ever been written or modified by an LLM, and none ever shall
 * be. The only authoritative documentation is found in the documents located
 * under `Documentation/OS`; where this Doxygen text does not match the current
 * source, the source is definitive, and anything unclear on review of the code
 * should be investigated before being relied upon.
 */
